      "text": "To determine the number of positive divisors of the number \\(24\\), we start by finding its prime factorization.\n\nFirst, we divide \\(24\\) by the smallest prime number, which is \\(2\\):\n\\[\n24 \\div 2 = 12\n\\]\nNext, we divide \\(12\\) by \\(2\\):\n\\[\n12 \\div 2 = 6\n\\]\nNext, we divide \\(6\\) by \\(2\\):\n\\[\n6 \\div 2 = 3\n\\]\nFinally, \\(3\\) is a prime number, so we stop here. Thus, the prime factorization of \\(24\\) is:\n\\[\n24 = 2^3 \\times 3^1\n\\]\n\nTo find the number of positive divisors, we use the formula that involves the exponents in the prime factorization. If a number \\(n\\) has a prime factorization of the form \\(p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\), then the number of positive divisors of \\(n\\) is given by:\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nFor \\(24 = 2^3 \\times 3^1\\), the exponents are \\(3\\) and \\(1\\). Applying the formula, we get:\n\\[\n(3 + 1)(1 + 1) = 4 \\times 2 = 8\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of positive divisors of \\(24\\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).\n\nTo verify, we list all the positive divisors of \\(24\\): \\(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24\\). Counting these, we see there are indeed \\(8\\) divisors.\n\nWhat is the value of x if x divided by 5.5 equals 3.4?\n\nTo find the value of \\( x \\) in the equation \\( x \\div 5.5 = 3.4 \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Start with the given equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{5.5} = 3.4\n   \\]\n\n2. To isolate \\( x \\), multiply both sides of the equation by 5.5:\n   \\[\n   x = 3.4 \\times 5.5\n   \\]\n\n3. Now, perform the multiplication on the right side. First, you can multiply 3.4 by 5.5 directly or convert 3.4 to a fraction to make the multiplication easier. Since \\( 3.4 = \\frac{34}{10} = \\frac{17}{5} \\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{17}{5} \\times 5.5\n   \\]\n\n4. To multiply, it is often easier to convert 5.5 to a fraction. Since \\( 5.5 = \\frac{11}{2} \\), the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{17}{5} \\times \\frac{11}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. Multiply the numerators together and the denominators together:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{17 \\times 11}{5 \\times 2} = \\frac{187}{10}\n   \\]\n\n6. Convert the fraction \\(\\frac{187}{10}\\) back to a decimal to simplify the division:\n   \\[\n   x = 18.7\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{18.7}\\).\n\nGiven that $a= \\int_{2}^{3} 3x^2dx$, find the coefficient of $x^{-3}$ in the expansion of the binomial $\\left( \\frac{1}{x} - a \\right)^6$.\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of \\( a \\) given by the integral \\( a = \\int_{2}^{3} 3x^2 \\, dx \\).\n\nWe start by evaluating the integral:\n\\[\na = \\int_{2}^{3} 3x^2 \\, dx\n\\]\nWe can factor out the constant 3:\n\\[\na = 3 \\int_{2}^{3} x^2 \\, dx\n\\]\nNext, we compute the integral of \\( x^2 \\):\n\\[\n\\int_{2}^{3} x^2 \\, dx = \\left[ \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_{2}^{3}\n\\]\nEvaluating this, we get:\n\\[\n\\left[ \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_{2}^{3} = \\frac{3^3}{3} - \\frac{2^3}{3} = \\frac{27}{3} - \\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{19}{3}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\na = 3 \\cdot \\frac{19}{3} = 19\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the coefficient of \\( x^{-3} \\) in the expansion of the binomial \\( \\left( \\frac{1}{x} - 19 \\right)^6 \\).\n\nUsing the binomial theorem, the expansion of \\( \\left( \\frac{1}{x} - 19 \\right)^6 \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{x} - 19 \\right)^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\binom{6}{k} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^{6-k} (-19)^k\n\\]\nWe need the term where the power of \\( x \\) is \\(-3\\). The general term in the expansion is:\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{k} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^{6-k} (-19)^k = \\binom{6}{k} (-19)^k x^{-(6-k)}\n\\]\nWe set the exponent of \\( x \\) to \\(-3\\):\n\\[\n-(6-k) = -3 \\implies k = 3\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( k = 3 \\) into the general term, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} (-19)^3 x^{-(6-3)} = \\binom{6}{3} (-19)^3 x^{-3}\n\\]\nWe calculate \\( \\binom{6}{3} \\):\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} = \\frac{6!}{3!3!